From: PwndaWallet <pwndamining@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] bids: a narrow, gated way to restart a swap the engine
 parked in BID_ERROR

Transport for PWNDA-PATCH-10's premise, the way 0004 is transport for 0003.

`BID_ERROR` is terminal for the adaptor-sig state machine. `checkXmrBidState`
has no branch for it -- unlike its non-XMR sibling `checkBidState`, which
carries an explicit `pass  # Wait for user input` -- so a bid that lands there
is never re-examined by any tick. When the underlying cause was transient the
swap itself is fine and only the TRACKING is stuck: the 2026-08-23 mainnet
case was a chain-A redeem that had already confirmed, rejected on rebroadcast
as a duplicate (PWNDA-PATCH-10 stops that specific cause from recurring; this
patch is how an already-stuck bid gets out).

Upstream's only exit is `manualBidUpdate`, which is reachable from the HTML
bid page alone -- no JSON route -- and takes an arbitrary operator-chosen
target state. An embedding host that wants to offer recovery has to either
drive a browser form or expose arbitrary state-setting to its own renderer.
Neither is acceptable: the second hands the renderer the ability to put any
bid into any state, including states that would re-publish a transaction.

`pwndaRecoverStalledBid` is the non-arbitrary version. It takes no target
state. It restores the ONE pairing the engine itself uses for this step --
`XMR_SWAP_LOCK_RELEASED` + `ActionTypes.REDEEM_XMR_SWAP_LOCK_TX_A`, exactly
as `processADSBidReversed` sets it -- and then lets `redeemXmrBidCoinALockTx`
decide what actually happens. That function already returns early if the
redeem is recorded or a chain-A refund exists, and with PWNDA-PATCH-10 it is
idempotent against an on-chain duplicate. So the worst case of calling this is
a wasted tick, never a double spend.

Three gates, all of which REFUSE rather than raise, so a caller can offer
recovery unconditionally and explain when it was not needed:

  1. the bid must actually be in `BID_ERROR` -- recovering a live bid would
     fight the state machine that still owns it;
  2. no chain-A refund may be in flight -- if the timelock path has taken
     over, re-queueing a redeem against it is the one genuinely unsafe thing
     this could do;
  3. a chain-A spend tx must exist -- there is nothing to retry otherwise.

Not an upstream-PR candidate: the endpoint exists because an embedding host
answers "who drives recovery" differently than a standalone node with an
operator at its web UI does. The underlying gap it works around -- no
`BID_ERROR` branch in `checkXmrBidState` -- is arguably an upstream bug, but
fixing that properly is a state-machine change, not an endpoint.

Marker: PWNDA-PATCH-11
